From 61b912676fa7a080c470db9de79610768e1f93fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:34:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amd/display: Automatic keeps full range RGB on a sink
 that selects it

linux-9970 (5342f7d98a26 of amd-staging-drm-next, backported) makes
Broadcast RGB "Automatic" send limited range RGB on the CTA modes of an
HDMI sink that the AVI InfoFrame names by their VIC (all but 640x480).
i915 does it on every CTA mode; the four 4K modes that DRM sends with an
HDMI VIC and VIC 0 in the AVI InfoFrame (3840x2160 at 24, 25 and 30 Hz,
4096x2160 at 24 Hz) are left out by linux-9970, and linux-9983 takes
them. It fixes sinks that cannot select the RGB range
(no QS in their Video Capability Data Block): there amdgpu sent full
range with the default quantization in the AVI InfoFrame, which such a
sink takes as limited, so shadows were crushed and highlights clipped.

On a sink that can select the range, Linux 7.2.11 already sends a
consistent signal: dm_helpers_parse_edid_caps() copies the QS bit to
edid_caps.qs_bit (the change of a56a5007452c), and set_avi_info_frame()
then signals Q = full for COLOR_SPACE_SRGB and
COLOR_SPACE_2020_RGB_FULLRANGE, and the levels of the framebuffer are
not compressed. linux-9970 would turn such sinks to limited range too:
the output CSC then compresses 0-255 to 16-235, and the formatter
reduces the result to the depth of the link (dithered at 8 bits by
default), so a full range picture keeps 220 of its 256 levels per
component at 8 bits. Nothing is wrong with the signal of today there,
and nothing needs fixing.

So Automatic follows the CTA-861 default only on a sink that cannot
select the range; on one that can, it stays full range, as before.
Full and Limited keep their meaning. The Dolby Vision tunnel
(linux-9910) sets Full itself and is not affected; nor are YCbCr
outputs, which are always limited range, nor DisplayPort and DVI sinks.

What a sink of each kind receives, from this change:
- HDMI sink without QS, CTA mode other than 640x480 (the four modes of
  an HDMI VIC with linux-9983): limited range, AVI Q = default
  (CTA-861: limited). Before linux-9970: full range
  with Q = default. This is the fix;
- HDMI sink with QS: full range, Q = full, as in 7.2.11;
- 640x480, DisplayPort, DVI: full range, as in 7.2.11.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c | 11 +++++++++--
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
index fbaab6f..936ca4e 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/display/amdgpu_dm/amdgpu_dm.c
@@ -7003,18 +7003,25 @@ get_aspect_ratio(const struct drm_display_mode *mode_in)
 /*
  * CTA-861 5.1: RGB video formats default to limited range except 640x480 (VIC 1).
  * With Broadcast RGB left at Automatic follow that default, as i915 does.
+ *
+ * Hades: except on a sink that can select the RGB range (QS in its Video Capability Data
+ * Block): there Automatic stays full range, and the AVI InfoFrame says so (Q = full,
+ * set_avi_info_frame() with stream->qs_bit), as before. That signal is consistent, and the
+ * framebuffer reaches the sink unchanged instead of being compressed to 16-235 and dithered.
  */
 static bool rgb_output_is_limited_range(const struct dc_crtc_timing *dc_crtc_timing,
 					const struct drm_connector_state *connector_state)
 {
+	const struct drm_display_info *info = &connector_state->connector->display_info;
+
 	switch (connector_state->hdmi.broadcast_rgb) {
 	case DRM_HDMI_BROADCAST_RGB_FULL:
 		return false;
 	case DRM_HDMI_BROADCAST_RGB_LIMITED:
 		return true;
 	default:
-		return connector_state->connector->display_info.is_hdmi &&
-		       dc_crtc_timing->vic > 1;
+		return info->is_hdmi && dc_crtc_timing->vic > 1 &&
+		       !info->rgb_quant_range_selectable;
 	}
 }
 
